// transientPowerLawRheology: the wall law 5.45 - 5.47 proved three times in the
// steady network is now the number the *transient* momentum row reads.
//
// Both ends are fixed-pressure nodes, so each pipe is its own one-row system:
//
//     rho L/A * dQ/dt = dp - dp_wall(Q),   dp = p(A) - p(B) = 1.0e4 Pa
//
// and dp_wall is the same function the steady kernel charges for the same wall
// (pipeFrictionPa). That makes the end of a transient run a cross-kernel
// identity rather than a coincidence: relaxed to rest, this row must land on
// the exact laminar discharge of the declared law -- the profile integral 5.47
// corrected 5.45 with, not the Metzner-Reed constant 5.45 first used,
//
//     Q = (pi d^3 / 8) * n/(3n+1) * (dp d / (4 K L))^(1/n),   tau_w = dp d/(4L)
//
// which the checker recomputes from the case file, not from the app. At
// n = 1, K = mu it is 128 mu L Q = pi d^4 dp.
//
// Before this line the transient row built `rho u d / mu` by hand. A case that
// declared `flowIndex 0.5` here therefore did not fail and did not warn: it ran
// Newtonian friction and reported the Newtonian rate. The six pipes below are
// one geometry and one drop with five different declarations, so the
// old behaviour would have made all six traces *identical* -- the increment is
// falsifiable in one run.
//
// What the exponents buy at dp = 1.0e4 Pa, L = 20 m, d = 0.02 m (fluid 850 kg/m3,
// mu 0.1 Pa.s, so the Newtonian rate is 1.96349e-5 m3/s):
//
//   * `Thin05` (n = 0.5, K = 1) and `Thin07` shear-thin: a much smaller rate
//     than the Newtonian one at the same drop, because K is not mu, and a
//     relaxation whose slope is the law's own n dp/Q rather than the chord dp/Q.
//   * `Thick13` (n = 1.3, K = 0.02) shear-thickens and is the only pipe here
//     whose steady rate beats the Newtonian one.
//   * `Newt` (undeclared) and `Decl1` (n = 1, K = mu) are the inertness control:
//     term for term the same algebra, so their traces must be bit-identical.
//   * `Thin05Rev` starts at a negative rate, where the law is the odd
//     continuation of itself -- a statement only a time-marching row can make.
//
// The generalized number is largest for `Thick13` at its own steady rate (32.2)
// and the Newtonian one is 10.6, so Churchill's laminar branch is in force in
// every reported state -- the checker walks all 2001 of them -- and the closed
// form is the exact laminar solution rather than a correlation. The turbulent
// half is reached by the generalized number and validated against nothing here.
//
// A threshold is refused in this kernel, not approximated: static hold has to be
// integrated in time and here the drop is what the row is solving for. See
// case_rejectYieldStress.dict and the three other refusal files.
//
// verified by tests/check_transient_power_law.py
// (ctest verify.transientPowerLawRheology).

fluid
{
    rho 850; mu 0.1;
}

nodes
{
    A { fixedPressure 3.0e4; }
    B { fixedPressure 2.0e4; }
}

pipes
{
    // --- the inertness pair: same geometry, one declared, one not ---
    Newt  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; }
    Decl1 { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02;
            flowIndex 1; consistency 0.1; }

    // --- the sign of the exponent, same geometry, starts at rest ---
    Thin05  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; initialFlow 0;
              flowIndex 0.5; consistency 1.0; }
    Thin07  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; initialFlow 0;
              flowIndex 0.7; consistency 2.0; }
    Thick13 { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; initialFlow 0;
              flowIndex 1.3; consistency 0.02; }

    // --- the odd continuation: released flowing the wrong way ---
    Thin05Rev { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; initialFlow -2.0e-6;
                flowIndex 0.5; consistency 1.0; }
}

time
{
    // dt has to be small against the *fastest* relaxation here (n = 0.7 damps
    // with tau = rho L/A / (n dp/Q) = 7.5 ms), so the tail the checker fits is
    // several samples long, and endTime has to be long against the slowest
    // (Newtonian, tau = 0.106 s) so "relaxed to rest" means what it says:
    // 37 of those time constants.
    dt 0.002;
    endTime 4.0;
    reportEvery 1;
}
